// Day 8 question pool: Domain 1, Design Secure Architectures. Data only; ids key saved stats, never rename or reuse.
window.QUIZ.push(

// ---------- Task 1.1: Design secure access to AWS resources ----------

{id:"q08-001", day:8, dom:1, tags:["IAM roles & cross-account","Containers"],
 q:"A logistics company runs eight microservices as Amazon ECS tasks on a shared cluster of EC2 container instances. Today the container instance IAM role carries the combined permissions of every service, so any compromised container can read every S3 bucket and DynamoDB table that any service uses. The security team wants each service to receive only the permissions it needs, without rebuilding the cluster. Which solution meets these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
 o:["Split the cluster so that each microservice runs in its own Auto Scaling group with a dedicated container instance role.",
    "Store a separate IAM user access key for each service in Secrets Manager and inject it as a container environment variable.",
    "Create an IAM task role per service with a least-privilege policy, reference it in each task definition, and trim the instance role.",
    "Attach a permissions boundary to the container instance role that limits it to the actions used by the most sensitive service."],
 a:[2],
 x:["Dedicated instances per service would isolate permissions but multiply the infrastructure to run and patch; ECS task roles give per-task credentials on the shared cluster.",
    "This reintroduces long-term access keys that must be rotated and protected, which is exactly what IAM roles exist to avoid.",
    "ECS task roles deliver separate temporary credentials to each task, so every service gets only its own permissions while the instance role keeps just what the ECS agent needs.",
    "A boundary on the shared role still gives every container the same permissions, so services would either lose access they need or keep access they should not have."],
 ref:["d8e2","d4b2"], doc:"ecs"},

{id:"q08-002", day:8, dom:1, tags:["KMS & encryption","SQS"],
 q:"S3 event notifications deliver new-object events to an Amazon SQS queue. After the security team enabled SSE-KMS on the queue with the AWS managed key for SQS, no more event messages arrive, although the queue's access policy still allows Amazon S3 to send messages. The queue must stay encrypted with a KMS key. What should a solutions architect do?",
 o:["Edit the key policy of the AWS managed key for SQS to allow the Amazon S3 service principal to use the key.",
    "Encrypt the queue with a customer managed key whose key policy allows Amazon S3 to use the key.",
    "Enable S3 Bucket Keys on the source bucket so that S3 needs fewer KMS requests to send each event.",
    "Add <code>kms:Decrypt</code> on the AWS managed key to the IAM role of the application that reads the queue."],
 a:[1],
 x:["The key policies of AWS managed keys cannot be modified, so Amazon S3 can never be allowed to use that key.",
    "S3 must be able to call <code>kms:GenerateDataKey</code> and <code>kms:Decrypt</code> on the queue's key, which is possible only with a customer managed key whose key policy allows the Amazon S3 service principal.",
    "Bucket Keys reduce KMS calls for objects stored in the bucket; they have nothing to do with S3 sending encrypted messages to SQS.",
    "The consumer's permissions are not the problem; S3 itself cannot encrypt messages for the queue, so the events never arrive."],
 ref:["d8e4","d4b4"], doc:"kms"},

{id:"q08-003", day:8, dom:1, tags:["KMS & encryption","S3 security"],
 q:"A company's S3 bucket holds 400 million existing objects encrypted with SSE-S3. A new policy requires every object, existing and future, to be encrypted with SSE-KMS using a specific customer managed key so that access can be audited and revoked through the key policy. The company wants the least operational effort and no changes to the applications that upload objects. Which combination of actions meets these requirements? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Change the bucket's default encryption to SSE-KMS with the customer managed key, and enable S3 Bucket Keys.",
    "Turn on automatic rotation for the AWS managed key for Amazon S3 so that the existing objects are re-encrypted.",
    "Run an S3 Batch Operations job that updates the encryption of each existing object to SSE-KMS with the customer managed key.",
    "Add a lifecycle rule that transitions the existing objects to S3 Standard-IA so that S3 re-encrypts them as they move.",
    "Enable S3 Versioning so that S3 creates a new SSE-KMS encrypted version of every existing object in the bucket."],
 a:[0,2],
 x:["Default encryption makes S3 encrypt every new upload with the customer managed key without application changes, and Bucket Keys reduce the number and cost of KMS requests.",
    "SSE-S3 objects do not use a KMS key at all, AWS managed keys already rotate automatically, and key rotation never re-encrypts existing objects.",
    "Default encryption affects only new objects; a Batch Operations update object encryption job changes existing SSE-S3 objects to SSE-KMS with the chosen key without copying any data and produces a completion report (older guidance used a Batch Operations copy job that rewrote each object in place).",
    "Lifecycle transitions change the storage class, not the encryption type, so the objects would stay encrypted with SSE-S3.",
    "Enabling versioning only keeps earlier versions when objects are later overwritten or deleted; it does not create new versions or change the encryption of existing objects."],
 ref:["d8e4","d4b4"], doc:"s3"},

{id:"q08-004", day:8, dom:1, tags:["IAM roles & cross-account"],
 q:"A manufacturing company runs scripts on servers in its own data centers that upload production data to Amazon S3. The scripts use IAM user access keys stored in configuration files, and an audit flagged these long-term credentials. The company already runs an internal public key infrastructure that issues an X.509 certificate to every server. The company wants the scripts to use short-lived AWS credentials with the LEAST operational overhead. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Store the access keys in AWS Secrets Manager with automatic rotation, and have the scripts fetch them at startup.",
    "Create IAM Identity Center users for the servers and have the scripts complete the portal sign-in every morning.",
    "Create an IAM role whose trust policy allows the servers' IP addresses, and have the scripts assume it without keys.",
    "Use IAM Roles Anywhere with a trust anchor for the company's CA so that servers trade certificates for role credentials."],
 a:[3],
 x:["The scripts would still need long-term credentials to call Secrets Manager, and rotated access keys are still long-term keys.",
    "IAM Identity Center sign-in is a browser-based flow for people; unattended scripts on servers cannot complete it every day.",
    "A trust policy names principals, not IP addresses, and calling AssumeRole requires existing AWS credentials, so the scripts cannot assume a role this way.",
    "IAM Roles Anywhere lets workloads outside AWS present X.509 certificates from a registered CA to obtain temporary credentials for an IAM role, so no access keys remain on the servers."],
 ref:["d8e2","d4b2"], doc:"iam"},

{id:"q08-005", day:8, dom:1, tags:["Organizations & SCPs"],
 q:"A healthcare company must ensure that workloads are deployed only in eu-west-1 and eu-central-1 to meet data residency rules. The rule must apply to every current and future member account in its organization, including users who have full administrator permissions in those accounts. Global services such as IAM and Route 53 must keep working. What should a solutions architect do?",
 o:["Attach an IAM policy to every user and role in each account that denies actions when <code>aws:RequestedRegion</code> is not an approved Region.",
    "Attach an SCP to the organization root that denies actions when <code>aws:RequestedRegion</code> is not an approved Region, exempting global services.",
    "Deploy an AWS Config rule in each account that detects resources outside the approved Regions and notifies the security team through SNS.",
    "Require a permissions boundary on every IAM role that limits actions to the two approved Regions and exempts the global services."],
 a:[1],
 x:["Account administrators can detach or edit IAM policies, and every new principal and account would need the policy added, so the control is neither central nor tamper-proof.",
    "An SCP at the root applies to every member account, including new ones and their administrators, and a deny on <code>aws:RequestedRegion</code> with exemptions for global services is the standard data residency guardrail.",
    "AWS Config is a detective control; it reports noncompliant resources after they are created instead of preventing them.",
    "Boundaries must be attached to each principal, and administrators could create principals without one, so this is not an organization-wide preventive guardrail."],
 ref:["d8e2","d4c2","d4w1"], doc:"organizations"},

{id:"q08-006", day:8, dom:1, tags:["Organizations & SCPs","IAM policies"],
 q:"In a company's organization, the FullAWSAccess SCP on the Analytics OU has been replaced with an SCP that allows only <code>s3:*</code> and <code>athena:*</code> actions. A developer in a member account under that OU has an identity-based policy that allows <code>dynamodb:*</code> and <code>s3:GetObject</code> on a reporting bucket. The developer reports that DynamoDB calls fail with AccessDenied. Which statement correctly describes the developer's effective permissions?",
 o:["The developer can use all S3, Athena and DynamoDB actions, because effective permissions are the union of the SCP and the identity-based policy.",
    "The developer can use all S3 and Athena actions, because the SCP grants those actions to every principal in the accounts under the OU.",
    "The developer can call only <code>s3:GetObject</code> on the reporting bucket, because only actions allowed by both the SCP and the identity policy are permitted.",
    "The developer can use DynamoDB and <code>s3:GetObject</code>, because SCPs restrict only the root user of each member account and not IAM principals."],
 a:[2],
 x:["SCPs and identity policies are not combined as a union; an action must be allowed by both to be permitted.",
    "SCPs never grant permissions; they define the maximum available, and the developer still needs an identity-based policy that allows each action.",
    "An action is allowed only if the SCP permits it and an identity-based policy allows it, so DynamoDB is blocked by the SCP and only <code>s3:GetObject</code> remains.",
    "SCPs apply to all IAM users and roles in member accounts as well as their root users; only the management account is unaffected by SCPs."],
 ref:["d8e2","d4c2","d4c1"], doc:"organizations"},

{id:"q08-007", day:8, dom:1, tags:["Organizations & SCPs","IAM policies"],
 q:"A company with 80 accounts in AWS Organizations requires every new EC2 instance to carry a CostCenter tag from the moment it is created. Launches without the tag must be blocked in all member accounts, including launches by account administrators, and the company does not want to clean up untagged instances after the fact. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Attach an SCP that denies <code>ec2:RunInstances</code> for instances when <code>aws:RequestTag/CostCenter</code> is null.",
    "Deploy the AWS Config required-tags rule with automatic remediation that terminates instances without the tag.",
    "Attach a permissions boundary to every IAM role in each account that requires the CostCenter tag on launches.",
    "Activate CostCenter as a cost allocation tag in the Billing console of the organization's management account."],
 a:[0],
 x:["An SCP applies to every principal in the member accounts, including administrators, and a Null condition on <code>aws:RequestTag/CostCenter</code> denies any launch request that does not include the tag.",
    "This Config rule detects untagged instances only after they launch, so they would run and then need remediation, which the company wants to avoid.",
    "Boundaries must be attached to each principal separately, and account administrators can create roles without them or remove them, so launches are not reliably blocked.",
    "Cost allocation tags only make existing tags usable in billing reports; they do not enforce tagging."],
 ref:["d8e2","d4c2"], doc:"organizations"},

{id:"q08-008", day:8, dom:1, tags:["KMS & encryption","IAM policies"],
 q:"A data science role reads objects from an S3 bucket that is encrypted with SSE-KMS using a customer managed key. The security team wants the role to be able to use the key only when Amazon S3 calls AWS KMS on its behalf to decrypt objects, so that the role cannot use the key directly through the KMS API or through any other AWS service. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Remove <code>kms:Decrypt</code> from the role so that S3 decrypts the objects with its own service permissions.",
    "Enable automatic rotation on the key so that any copies used outside Amazon S3 stop working every year.",
    "Add an S3 bucket policy that denies <code>kms:Decrypt</code> to the role for any request not made through S3.",
    "Allow <code>kms:Decrypt</code> for the role in the key policy only with a <code>kms:ViaService</code> condition for S3."],
 a:[3],
 x:["SSE-KMS decryption is authorized with the caller's permissions, so without <code>kms:Decrypt</code> the role could not read the objects at all.",
    "Rotation keeps older key material available for decryption and does not restrict which service the key is used through.",
    "A bucket policy controls S3 actions on the bucket; it cannot govern KMS API permissions.",
    "The <code>kms:ViaService</code> condition key limits use of the key to requests that the named service makes on the principal's behalf, written as <code>s3.eu-west-1.amazonaws.com</code>, so direct KMS calls by the role are denied."],
 ref:["d8e4","d4c1","d4b4"], doc:"kms"},

{id:"q08-009", day:8, dom:1, tags:["S3 security","VPC endpoints & PrivateLink"],
 q:"A financial services company stores regulated reports in an S3 bucket. The applications that read the reports run on EC2 instances in private subnets with no internet access. Compliance requires that traffic to S3 does not traverse the internet and that the bucket rejects any request that does not arrive through the company's VPC, even from users with valid IAM permissions. Which combination of steps meets these requirements? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Add a NAT gateway in a public subnet and route the private subnets' S3 traffic through it.",
    "Create an S3 gateway VPC endpoint and associate it with the route tables of the private subnets.",
    "Add a bucket policy that denies all requests unless <code>aws:SourceIp</code> is within the VPC's private CIDR range.",
    "Add a bucket policy that denies all requests unless <code>aws:SourceVpce</code> equals the ID of the VPC endpoint.",
    "Turn on S3 Block Public Access at the account level and require SSE-KMS default encryption on the bucket."],
 a:[1,3],
 x:["A NAT gateway needs an internet gateway and sends requests to the public S3 endpoints, so they do not arrive through a VPC endpoint that the bucket policy can require, and it adds hourly and per-GB charges.",
    "A gateway endpoint adds an S3 prefix-list route to the private route tables, so traffic reaches S3 over the AWS network with no internet path and no NAT gateway.",
    "Requests that arrive through a VPC endpoint do not populate <code>aws:SourceIp</code> (the private address is in <code>aws:VpcSourceIp</code>), so a private CIDR check on it cannot identify traffic from the VPC.",
    "An explicit deny on <code>aws:SourceVpce</code> rejects every request that does not come through that endpoint, even when the caller's IAM policies allow it.",
    "Block Public Access stops public access and SSE-KMS encrypts data, but neither restricts authenticated IAM principals to requests from the VPC."],
 ref:["d8e2","d2c3","d4c1"], doc:"s3"},

{id:"q08-010", day:8, dom:1, tags:["Root user & MFA","IAM policies"],
 q:"A company still uses IAM users for a small operations team that works in both the AWS Management Console and the AWS CLI. A new policy states that stopping or terminating production EC2 instances must be possible only from sessions authenticated with MFA, including CLI sessions. MFA devices are already registered for every user. What should a solutions architect do to enforce this requirement?",
 o:["Enable MFA on the root user and require the team to sign in as root whenever they need to stop or terminate production instances.",
    "Deny <code>ec2:StopInstances</code> and <code>ec2:TerminateInstances</code> unless <code>aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent</code> is true, and have CLI users get MFA-backed session credentials.",
    "Rely on the registered MFA devices, because once a device is assigned, AWS requires an MFA code for every API call made with that user's access keys.",
    "Deploy the AWS Config managed rule that checks that MFA is enabled for IAM users, with automatic remediation that disables noncompliant users."],
 a:[1],
 x:["Using the root user for routine operations violates best practice and grants far more power than needed; root is reserved for the few tasks that require it.",
    "The key is true only in MFA-authenticated sessions, so the explicit deny (written with BoolIfExists so requests that lack the key are denied too) blocks calls made without MFA; <code>aws sts get-session-token</code> with an MFA code gives the CLI MFA-backed credentials.",
    "A registered device enforces MFA only at console sign-in; API calls signed with long-term access keys are not MFA-authenticated unless a policy requires it.",
    "The rule checks only that devices are registered, which is already true; it does not require MFA at the moment the stop or terminate calls are made."],
 ref:["d8e2","d4c1","d1b1"], doc:"iam"},

{id:"q08-011", day:8, dom:1, tags:["Root user & MFA","IAM Identity Center & federation"],
 q:"A startup has just created its first and only AWS account. The founder signed up with the root user and has been using it for daily work, including deploying resources from the AWS CLI with a root access key. An auditor recommends aligning with AWS best practices for protecting the root user while keeping day-to-day administration possible. Which combination of actions should the company take? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Keep the root access key but rotate it every 90 days and store it in AWS Secrets Manager between uses.",
    "Create one IAM user with AdministratorAccess whose password the whole team shares for daily work.",
    "Delete the root user's access key, enable MFA on the root user, and use root only for tasks that require it.",
    "Attach an SCP to the account that denies all actions by the root user so that root can never be used again.",
    "Enable IAM Identity Center and give administrators a permission set with MFA for their daily work."],
 a:[2,4],
 x:["Root access keys should be deleted, not rotated; storing one anywhere keeps alive an unrestricted long-term credential that IAM policies cannot limit.",
    "Shared credentials remove individual accountability in CloudTrail and violate least privilege; every person should have their own identity.",
    "Root access keys should not exist, and MFA on the root user plus reserving it for root-only tasks, such as changing account settings, is the core root protection guidance.",
    "SCPs never apply to a standalone account or to an organization's management account, and root must remain usable for the few tasks that only root can perform.",
    "IAM Identity Center gives each administrator an individual identity with MFA and short-lived credentials, replacing root for daily administration."],
 ref:["d8e2","d1b1","d1c1"], doc:"iam"},

{id:"q08-012", day:8, dom:1, tags:["IAM roles & cross-account","S3 security"],
 q:"An analytics application runs on EC2 instances in Account B and writes results to a DynamoDB table in Account B. It must also read source files from an S3 bucket owned by Account A, which uses SSE-S3 encryption. The developers want the application to use the single set of credentials from its instance role for both tasks instead of switching between roles. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Create a role in Account A that the instance role assumes before each S3 read, and switch back to the instance role before writing to DynamoDB.",
    "Add a bucket policy in Account A that grants the Account B instance role <code>s3:GetObject</code>, and allow that action on the bucket in the role's policy.",
    "Configure S3 replication from Account A's bucket to a bucket in Account B, and grant the instance role read access to the replica bucket.",
    "Attach an SCP to Account B that allows <code>s3:GetObject</code> on Account A's bucket, and enable ACLs on the bucket with bucket-owner-full-control."],
 a:[1],
 x:["While using an assumed role's credentials the caller has only that role's permissions, so the application would have to juggle two credential sets, which the developers want to avoid.",
    "With a resource-based bucket policy, the instance role keeps its own permissions and is also allowed by Account A, so one set of credentials covers both the S3 read and the DynamoDB write.",
    "Replication adds a second copy of the data, extra cost and lag, and is unnecessary when a bucket policy can grant direct cross-account access.",
    "SCPs cannot grant permissions, and ACLs are disabled by default and not recommended; bucket-owner-full-control governs object ownership on upload, not cross-account reads."],
 ref:["d8e2","d4c1","d8c2"], doc:"s3"},

{id:"q08-013", day:8, dom:1, tags:["IAM policies"],
 q:"A research organization runs dozens of projects in one AWS account, and new projects start every month. Researchers federate into AWS through an identity provider that passes each user's project as a session tag. Each researcher must be able to manage only the EC2 instances and Secrets Manager secrets that belong to their own project. The security team wants to avoid writing or updating policies when projects are added. Which approach meets these requirements?",
 o:["Tag resources with their project, and allow actions only when <code>aws:ResourceTag/project</code> matches <code>aws:PrincipalTag/project</code> in one shared policy.",
    "Create a customer managed policy for every project that lists the ARNs of that project's resources, and attach it to a separate role per project.",
    "Create an IAM group for each project with a policy scoped to the project's resources, and add each researcher to the matching IAM group.",
    "Move each project into its own OU and attach an SCP that allows access only to resources whose ARNs contain the name of the project."],
 a:[0],
 x:["This is attribute-based access control: one policy compares the principal's session tag with the resource tag, so new projects need only correctly tagged users and resources, with no policy changes.",
    "Per-project policies work but must be written and updated whenever projects or resources change, which the team wants to avoid.",
    "IAM groups contain only IAM users, so federated sessions cannot be members, and per-project groups still need a new policy for every project.",
    "All projects share a single account, so OUs do not apply, SCPs cannot grant access, and this would still require a policy per project."],
 ref:["d8e2","d4b1","d4c1"], doc:"iam"},

{id:"q08-014", day:8, dom:1, tags:["IAM policies","Governance"],
 q:"After a merger, a company's security team must find out which S3 buckets, KMS keys, IAM roles, Lambda functions and SQS queues across all accounts in its organization can be accessed by principals outside the organization. The team wants continuous monitoring so that new external sharing is flagged automatically, with the least effort. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Enable Amazon Macie in every account and review its bucket-level findings about shared and publicly accessible buckets.",
    "Enable Amazon GuardDuty across the organization and review the findings that report unusual access from external accounts.",
    "Run the AWS Trusted Advisor security checks in every account each week and export the results to a central S3 bucket.",
    "Create an IAM Access Analyzer external access analyzer with the organization as the zone of trust in a delegated administrator account."],
 a:[3],
 x:["Macie inventories S3 buckets and discovers sensitive data, but it does not evaluate KMS keys, IAM role trust policies, Lambda functions or SQS queues.",
    "GuardDuty detects threats from activity logs; it does not analyze policies to show which resources can be accessed by external principals.",
    "Trusted Advisor's checks cover only a few resource types, run on a schedule rather than continuously, and do not use the organization as a trust boundary.",
    "Access Analyzer analyzes resource-based and trust policies and continuously reports any supported resource that is shared outside the zone of trust, here the whole organization."],
 ref:["d8e2","d4c1"], doc:"iam"},

{id:"q08-015", day:8, dom:1, tags:["Route 53","WAF, Shield & Firewall Manager"],
 q:"A security review found malware on an EC2 instance that resolved the domains of known command-and-control servers. The company wants to block DNS queries from all of its VPCs to known malicious domains, using threat domain lists that AWS keeps up to date, and to log every blocked query. The instances use the default Route 53 Resolver for DNS. Which solution meets these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
 o:["Add outbound network ACL rules that deny UDP and TCP port 53 to the IP addresses of the malicious DNS servers.",
    "Enable Amazon GuardDuty in every account so that it detects DNS queries to malicious domains and blocks them.",
    "Create a Route 53 Resolver DNS Firewall rule group with AWS managed domain lists and associate it with the VPCs.",
    "Run a self-managed DNS proxy on EC2 with a blocklist and point each VPC's DHCP options set at the proxy instances."],
 a:[2],
 x:["The instances send their queries to the Route 53 Resolver inside the VPC, not directly to the attackers' servers, so IP-based NACL rules cannot block lookups of malicious domains.",
    "GuardDuty analyzes DNS activity and reports findings about malicious domains, but it does not block the queries.",
    "DNS Firewall filters queries that pass through the Route 53 Resolver, can block every domain in AWS managed threat lists, and blocked queries are recorded through Resolver query logging.",
    "A self-managed proxy would work, but the company would have to run, patch, scale, and update it and its blocklists, which is far more overhead."],
 ref:["d8e3","d2w1"], doc:"route53"},

{id:"q08-016", day:8, dom:1, tags:["Governance","Organizations & SCPs"],
 q:"A fast-growing company plans to move from a single AWS account to dozens of accounts. It wants a landing zone with dedicated log archive and audit accounts, organization-wide CloudTrail logging, preventive and detective guardrails, and a self-service way to create new accounts that are compliant from the start. The company has a small cloud team. Which solution meets these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
 o:["Create an organization in AWS Organizations, write SCPs and AWS Config rules by hand, and baseline new accounts with CloudFormation StackSets.",
    "Enable AWS Security Hub CSPM with the AWS Foundational Security Best Practices standard and invite each new account as a member.",
    "Set up AWS Control Tower to create the landing zone, enable its controls, and use Account Factory to provision new accounts.",
    "Deploy AWS Config conformance packs in the current account and copy its IAM roles to every new account with a script."],
 a:[2],
 x:["This can produce a similar result, but building and maintaining the landing zone, guardrails and account baselines yourself is significant ongoing work for a small team.",
    "Security Hub CSPM (formerly AWS Security Hub) runs posture checks and aggregates findings, but it does not create accounts, set up log archive and audit accounts, or apply preventive guardrails.",
    "Control Tower automates a multi-account landing zone with log archive and audit accounts, an organization trail, preventive and detective controls, and Account Factory for compliant account vending.",
    "Conformance packs are detective rules only; they do not create accounts, centralize logging or enforce preventive controls, and copying roles by script is fragile."],
 ref:["d8e2","d6w2","d4c2"], doc:"controltower"},

{id:"q08-017", day:8, dom:1, tags:["IAM roles & cross-account"],
 q:"A developer accidentally pasted temporary credentials for an IAM role into a public forum. Many developers assume the same IAM role through a SAML 2.0 identity provider every day, and the leaked credentials remain valid for several more hours. The security team must immediately make every session of the role that was issued before now unusable, while developers who sign in again can keep working. What should the security team do?",
 o:["Reduce the role's maximum session duration to one hour so that the leaked credentials expire sooner than they otherwise would.",
    "Rotate the role's access keys from the IAM console so that the leaked keys stop working and new sessions receive the new keys.",
    "Detach all permission policies from the role until the leaked credentials expire, and then reattach the same policies afterwards.",
    "Use the revoke active sessions option on the role, which denies all actions to sessions whose credentials were issued before now."],
 a:[3],
 x:["The maximum session duration applies only to sessions requested after the change; credentials already issued keep their original expiration.",
    "Roles have no access keys to rotate; their temporary credentials come from AWS STS and stay valid until they expire unless the sessions are revoked.",
    "Removing permissions would block every developer, including those with fresh sessions, for hours, which conflicts with letting people keep working.",
    "Revoking sessions attaches an inline policy that denies all actions when <code>aws:TokenIssueTime</code> is earlier than the revocation time (plus about 30 seconds to allow for propagation), so leaked credentials stop working while sessions started after that are unaffected."],
 ref:["d8e2","d4b3"], doc:"iam"},

// ---------- Task 1.2: Design secure workloads and applications ----------

{id:"q08-018", day:8, dom:1, tags:["Security groups & NACLs"],
 q:"A company runs a three-tier application: an internet-facing ALB, an application tier on EC2 instances in an Auto Scaling group, and an Amazon RDS for MySQL database in private subnets. The private subnets also host batch-processing instances that must not reach the database. Application instances are replaced frequently, so their IP addresses change. How should a solutions architect allow database access only from the application tier with the least ongoing maintenance?",
 o:["Add a network ACL rule on the database subnets that allows TCP 3306 from the CIDR blocks of the subnets where the application instances run.",
    "Add an inbound rule to the database security group that allows TCP 3306 from the security group attached to the application instances.",
    "Add an inbound rule to the database security group that allows TCP 3306 from the private IP addresses of the current application instances.",
    "Add an inbound rule to the database security group that allows TCP 3306 from the security group that is attached to the load balancer."],
 a:[1],
 x:["Subnet-wide CIDR rules would also admit the batch instances that share those subnets, and stateless network ACLs need extra rules for return traffic.",
    "Referencing the application tier's security group admits any instance that carries that group, whatever its IP address, and excludes the batch instances.",
    "IP-based rules break every time the Auto Scaling group replaces an instance, which creates constant maintenance.",
    "The ALB connects to the application tier, not the database, so this would open the database to the wrong tier and still block the application instances."],
 ref:["d8e3","d2c1","d2w1"], doc:"vpc"},

{id:"q08-019", day:8, dom:1, tags:["Security groups & NACLs","WAF, Shield & Firewall Manager"],
 q:"A gaming company runs a UDP game server fleet on EC2 instances in public subnets behind a Network Load Balancer. The operations team has identified a single /24 CIDR range that is sending malicious traffic and must block it immediately for all instances in those subnets while legitimate players stay connected. Which solution meets this requirement?",
 o:["Create an AWS WAF web ACL with an IP set rule that blocks the CIDR range, and associate the web ACL with the Network Load Balancer.",
    "Add an inbound rule with a deny action for the CIDR range to the security group that is attached to the game server instances.",
    "Add an inbound deny rule for the CIDR range to the subnets' network ACL with a rule number lower than the rule that allows players.",
    "Enable AWS Shield Standard on the Network Load Balancer, and add the CIDR range to its list of blocked source addresses."],
 a:[2],
 x:["AWS WAF inspects HTTP(S) requests and attaches to resources such as CloudFront, ALB and API Gateway, not to Network Load Balancers or UDP traffic.",
    "Security groups support only allow rules, so a specific range cannot be denied with a security group.",
    "Network ACLs support explicit deny rules and evaluate rules in ascending order, so a lower-numbered deny blocks the range before the allow rule is reached.",
    "Shield Standard is always on and has no customer-managed block lists; it protects against common layer 3 and 4 DDoS attacks, not specific IP ranges."],
 ref:["d8e3","d2c1","d2b4"], doc:"vpc"},

{id:"q08-020", day:8, dom:1, tags:["Security groups & NACLs"],
 q:"A solutions architect replaced the default network ACL on a public subnet with a custom network ACL. It allows inbound TCP 443 from 0.0.0.0/0 and outbound TCP 443 to 0.0.0.0/0, and nothing else. The web servers in the subnet have security groups that allow inbound 443 and all outbound traffic. Users now cannot load the site, and the servers can no longer download updates from HTTPS repositories on the internet. Which changes will fix both problems? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Add an outbound network ACL rule that allows TCP ports 1024–65535 to 0.0.0.0/0.",
    "Add an outbound security group rule that allows TCP ports 1024–65535 to 0.0.0.0/0.",
    "Add an inbound network ACL rule that allows TCP port 80 from 0.0.0.0/0.",
    "Add an inbound network ACL rule that allows TCP ports 1024–65535 from 0.0.0.0/0.",
    "Add an inbound security group rule that allows TCP ports 1024–65535 from 0.0.0.0/0."],
 a:[0,3],
 x:["Network ACLs are stateless, so the responses to users, which go to the clients' ephemeral ports, need an explicit outbound rule.",
    "Security groups are stateful and already allow all outbound traffic; the block is in the network ACL.",
    "The site and the repositories both use HTTPS on port 443, so opening port 80 fixes neither problem.",
    "When the servers start HTTPS connections, the replies return to the servers' ephemeral ports, which the stateless network ACL must allow inbound.",
    "Security groups automatically allow return traffic for connections the instance starts, so this rule is unnecessary and the network ACL would still block the replies."],
 ref:["d8e3","d2b4","d2c1"], doc:"vpc"},

{id:"q08-021", day:8, dom:1, tags:["VPC endpoints & PrivateLink"],
 q:"A company runs Lambda functions and EC2 instances in private subnets of a VPC that has no internet gateway or NAT gateway. The workloads must now read secrets from AWS Secrets Manager and send messages to Amazon SQS. The security policy forbids any path to the internet from this VPC. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Create interface VPC endpoints for Secrets Manager and SQS with private DNS, and allow HTTPS from the workloads in the endpoint security group.",
    "Create gateway VPC endpoints for Secrets Manager and SQS, and add the endpoints to the route tables of the private subnets.",
    "Add a NAT gateway in a new public subnet, and restrict its security group to the Secrets Manager and SQS public endpoints.",
    "Peer the VPC with the AWS-managed service VPC for Secrets Manager and SQS, and add routes to the peering connection."],
 a:[0],
 x:["Interface endpoints (AWS PrivateLink) place private IP addresses in the subnets, private DNS resolves the normal service hostnames to them, and traffic never leaves the AWS network.",
    "Gateway endpoints exist only for Amazon S3 and DynamoDB; Secrets Manager and SQS are reached privately through interface endpoints.",
    "A NAT gateway creates an internet path, which the policy forbids, and NAT gateways do not have security groups.",
    "Customers cannot peer with AWS service VPCs; private access to AWS services is provided through VPC endpoints."],
 ref:["d8e3","d2c3","d8b1"], doc:"vpc"},

{id:"q08-022", day:8, dom:1, tags:["VPC endpoints & PrivateLink","S3 security"],
 q:"A pharmaceutical company's EC2 instances in private subnets access Amazon S3 through a gateway VPC endpoint. A data loss prevention review found that a compromised instance could copy research data to any S3 bucket in any AWS account, because the endpoint allows all S3 access. The company must allow access only to buckets owned by its own organization, with minimal impact on existing workloads. What should a solutions architect do?",
 o:["Add bucket policies to all company buckets that deny any request unless it comes through the company's VPC endpoint.",
    "Restrict the instances' security group outbound rules to the S3 managed prefix list for the Region on port 443.",
    "Attach a VPC endpoint policy that allows S3 actions only on resources whose <code>aws:ResourceOrgID</code> is the company's.",
    "Enable Amazon Macie on all company buckets to detect research data and alert when objects are copied elsewhere."],
 a:[2],
 x:["Bucket policies control who can reach the company's own buckets; they cannot stop an instance from writing to a bucket in someone else's account.",
    "The prefix list covers all of S3 in the Region, including buckets in other accounts, so it does not prevent exfiltration.",
    "An endpoint policy filters every request that passes through the endpoint, and the <code>aws:ResourceOrgID</code> condition limits requests to buckets in the company's organization.",
    "Macie discovers sensitive data in the company's own buckets but cannot see or block copies written to external buckets."],
 ref:["d8e3","d2c3","d8b1"], doc:"vpc"},

{id:"q08-023", day:8, dom:1, tags:["WAF, Shield & Firewall Manager","API Gateway"],
 q:"A fintech startup exposes a public REST API through an Amazon API Gateway Regional endpoint. The API is receiving SQL injection attempts and bursts of requests from thousands of rotating IP addresses that try to scrape data. The team wants to block common injection attacks and cap the number of requests any single IP address can send in a five-minute window, without writing custom code. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Create API Gateway usage plans with throttling limits, and require every client to send an API key with each request.",
    "Write a Lambda authorizer that counts requests per source IP in DynamoDB and rejects requests that contain SQL keywords.",
    "Rely on AWS Shield Standard, which is enabled automatically and filters SQL injection and request floods at the API stage.",
    "Associate an AWS WAF web ACL with the API stage that uses AWS Managed Rules for SQL injection and a rate-based rule."],
 a:[3],
 x:["Usage plans throttle per API key, not per IP address, and API keys are identifiers rather than a security control; they do not inspect requests for SQL injection.",
    "This is custom code to build and maintain, adds latency and cost to every call, and keyword matching is far weaker than managed rule groups.",
    "Shield Standard protects against common network and transport layer DDoS attacks; it does not inspect HTTP requests for SQL injection or rate-limit per IP address.",
    "AWS WAF integrates directly with API Gateway stages; managed rule groups block common injection patterns, and a rate-based rule blocks IP addresses that exceed the threshold in the evaluation window."],
 ref:["d8e3","d8c3","d4c3"], doc:"waf"},

{id:"q08-024", day:8, dom:1, tags:["VPC & subnets","Threat detection"],
 q:"A security operations team runs an intrusion detection appliance that must analyze the full packet contents of traffic to and from a group of EC2 instances that process payments. The team cannot install agents on the instances, and VPC Flow Logs do not contain the information that the appliance needs. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Configure VPC Traffic Mirroring on the instances' network interfaces and send the copies to the appliance.",
    "Enable VPC Flow Logs with all available fields and deliver them to the appliance through Amazon S3.",
    "Enable Amazon GuardDuty in the account so that it inspects packet payloads for the payment instances.",
    "Route the instances' traffic through a NAT gateway and turn on NAT gateway logging for packet capture."],
 a:[0],
 x:["Traffic Mirroring copies packets, including their payloads, from the elastic network interfaces of supported instances to a target such as an appliance or a Network Load Balancer, with no agent on the instances.",
    "Flow Logs record metadata such as addresses, ports, and byte counts, never packet contents, even with every field enabled.",
    "GuardDuty analyzes logs and events to produce findings; it does not perform full packet inspection or forward packets to the team's appliance.",
    "NAT gateways do not offer packet capture, and only internet-bound traffic would pass through one."],
 ref:["d8e3","d2w1"], doc:"vpc"},

{id:"q08-025", day:8, dom:1, tags:["Threat detection","S3 security"],
 q:"A law firm's web portal lets clients upload documents into an S3 bucket, and an internal workflow processes each new object. The firm must scan every uploaded object for malware before the workflow reads it, and it does not want to run or update antivirus software itself. Which solution meets these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
 o:["Enable Amazon Macie on the bucket and process only the objects for which Macie reports no sensitive data findings.",
    "Enable GuardDuty Malware Protection for S3 on the bucket and process only the objects whose scan result is clean.",
    "Activate Amazon Inspector for the account so that it scans each new object in the bucket for malicious content.",
    "Run an open-source antivirus engine in a Lambda function on each upload and update its signatures on a schedule."],
 a:[1],
 x:["Macie discovers sensitive data such as personal information; it does not detect malware.",
    "Malware Protection for S3 automatically scans newly uploaded objects in the selected bucket, publishes the results to EventBridge, and can tag each object with its scan status, so the workflow acts only on clean objects.",
    "Inspector scans EC2 instances, container images, and Lambda functions for software vulnerabilities, not S3 objects for malware.",
    "A Lambda-based scanner can work, but the firm would have to package, update, and scale the antivirus engine and its signatures, which it wants to avoid."],
 ref:["d8e3","d4c3"], doc:"guardduty"},

{id:"q08-026", day:8, dom:1, tags:["Threat detection","S3 security"],
 q:"A healthcare provider has thousands of S3 buckets that accumulated over years of projects. Before a compliance audit, it must identify which buckets contain personally identifiable information and protected health information. It also wants ongoing automated discovery as new data arrives, without building custom scanning code. Which AWS service should a solutions architect recommend?",
 o:["Amazon Macie, with automated sensitive data discovery enabled for the S3 buckets in the accounts.",
    "Amazon Inspector, configured to scan the buckets and report objects that contain sensitive personal data.",
    "Amazon GuardDuty with S3 Protection, configured to classify objects that contain sensitive personal data.",
    "AWS Config, with a custom rule that opens each new object and checks it for sensitive personal data."],
 a:[0],
 x:["Macie uses machine learning and pattern matching to discover sensitive data such as PII in S3, and automated discovery continuously samples buckets without custom code.",
    "Inspector finds software vulnerabilities and network exposure in EC2 instances, container images and Lambda functions; it does not classify data in S3.",
    "GuardDuty S3 Protection detects suspicious access patterns and GuardDuty Malware Protection for S3 scans uploads for malware, but neither classifies objects as PII or PHI.",
    "This requires writing and running custom code that reads every object, which the company wants to avoid; Config evaluates resource configuration, not object contents."],
 ref:["d8e3","d4c3"]},

{id:"q08-027", day:8, dom:1, tags:["RDS","IAM policies"],
 q:"An application on EC2 instances connects to an Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL database with a username and password. The security team wants to remove database passwords from the application entirely, so that database access is controlled through the instances' IAM role and authentication uses short-lived credentials. Connections must remain encrypted. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Enable IAM database authentication and have the application connect with an authentication token for its role.",
    "Store the password in AWS Secrets Manager with automatic rotation and retrieve it each time the application connects.",
    "Set the <code>rds.force_ssl</code> parameter so that every connection uses TLS, and keep using the existing password.",
    "Encrypt the DB instance with a KMS customer managed key and grant the instance role permission to use that key."],
 a:[0],
 x:["With IAM database authentication, the application uses its role to generate a token that is valid for 15 minutes instead of a password, access is granted through IAM policies, and connections use SSL/TLS.",
    "Rotation improves password hygiene, but the application still authenticates with a database password, which the team wants to remove.",
    "Forcing TLS encrypts the connections, but the application still uses a long-lived password.",
    "Encryption at rest protects stored data; it does not change how the application authenticates to the database."],
 ref:["d8e2","d4b2"], doc:"rds"},

{id:"q08-028", day:8, dom:1, tags:["Cognito"],
 q:"A startup is building a mobile photo-sharing app. Users must be able to sign up with an email address or sign in with their Google accounts, and the app must then upload photos directly to an S3 bucket, with each user limited to their own prefix in the bucket. No long-term AWS credentials may be embedded in the app. Which combination of steps meets these requirements? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Create an IAM user for each app user at sign-up and return that user's access key to the mobile app.",
    "Create an Amazon Cognito user pool for sign-up and sign-in, and add Google as a federated identity provider.",
    "Enable IAM Identity Center with Google as the identity source and assign each app user a permission set for S3.",
    "Create a Cognito identity pool that exchanges user pool tokens for temporary credentials scoped to each user's prefix.",
    "Embed one IAM access key that allows <code>s3:PutObject</code> in the app and keep it in the device's secure keychain."],
 a:[1,3],
 x:["IAM users do not scale to app customers, are subject to account quotas, and handing out long-term access keys violates the requirement.",
    "User pools provide the user directory, sign-up and sign-in, including federation with social providers such as Google, and issue tokens to the app.",
    "IAM Identity Center is for workforce access to AWS accounts and business applications, not for customer sign-up in a mobile app.",
    "Identity pools exchange the authenticated token for temporary AWS credentials, and a policy variable for the identity ID in the S3 resource ARN limits each user to their own prefix.",
    "A shared embedded key is a long-term credential that can be extracted from the app and would let anyone write wherever the key allows."],
 ref:["d8e3","d8c1"]},

{id:"q08-029", day:8, dom:1, tags:["Encryption in transit","Load balancing"],
 q:"A company's public website runs behind an Application Load Balancer that uses a TLS certificate bought from a third-party certificate authority and imported into AWS Certificate Manager. Last month the certificate expired unnoticed and the site was unavailable for hours. The company wants the certificate for its public domain to renew automatically from now on, with the LEAST operational effort. What should a solutions architect do?",
 o:["Re-import the third-party certificate into ACM and turn on ACM managed renewal for the imported certificate.",
    "Write a Lambda function that checks the certificate's expiry date daily and emails the team before it expires.",
    "Move TLS termination to the EC2 instances and renew certificates there with a cron job that calls the CA's API.",
    "Request a public certificate from ACM with DNS validation, keep the validation record, and use it on the listener."],
 a:[3],
 x:["ACM cannot renew imported certificates; only certificates that ACM issues are renewed automatically.",
    "A reminder helps, but someone must still buy, import, and deploy each new certificate, so renewal stays manual.",
    "Terminating TLS on the instances adds certificate management on every instance and custom automation, which increases operational effort.",
    "ACM renews the public certificates it issues automatically as long as the DNS validation CNAME record stays in place, and it deploys the renewed certificate to the ALB."],
 ref:["d8e4","d8c3"], doc:"acm"},

{id:"q08-030", day:8, dom:1, tags:["VPC & subnets","Internet & NAT gateways"],
 q:"A government contractor must control outbound traffic from workloads in private subnets that reach the internet through NAT gateways. Instances may connect only to an approved list of domain names, and all traffic leaving the VPC must also pass through signature-based intrusion prevention rules. The solution must use a managed AWS service. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Configure the instances' security groups with outbound rules that allow only the approved domain names on port 443.",
    "Use Route 53 Resolver DNS Firewall to allow DNS queries only for the approved domains and block all other domains.",
    "Associate an AWS WAF web ACL with the NAT gateways that uses a domain allow list and managed intrusion rule groups.",
    "Deploy AWS Network Firewall in dedicated subnets, route egress through its endpoints, and use domain and Suricata rules."],
 a:[3],
 x:["Security group rules accept only CIDR ranges, prefix lists or security groups, not domain names, and they cannot perform intrusion prevention.",
    "DNS Firewall filters DNS lookups, which helps with domain control, but it cannot inspect traffic for intrusion signatures and does not stop connections made directly to IP addresses.",
    "AWS WAF inspects inbound web requests on resources such as CloudFront, ALB and API Gateway; it cannot be associated with NAT gateways.",
    "Network Firewall is a managed stateful firewall and IPS for VPC traffic; routing egress through its endpoints enables domain allow lists and Suricata-compatible intrusion prevention rules."],
 ref:["d8e3","d2w1"]},

{id:"q08-031", day:8, dom:1, tags:["CloudFront","S3 security"],
 q:"A company hosts a single-page application in an S3 bucket that is encrypted with SSE-KMS using a customer managed key, and serves it through Amazon CloudFront. The security team requires that the bucket stay private with S3 Block Public Access enabled and that users can reach the content only through the CloudFront distribution. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Configure the bucket as a static website endpoint, use it as a custom origin, and allow only requests with a secret Referer header.",
    "Create an origin access identity (OAI), grant it <code>s3:GetObject</code> in the bucket policy, and add the OAI to the KMS key policy.",
    "Create an origin access control (OAC), allow this distribution in the bucket policy, and allow it to decrypt in the KMS key policy.",
    "Use CloudFront signed URLs for all objects, and grant the CloudFront key group <code>s3:GetObject</code> in the bucket policy."],
 a:[2],
 x:["Website endpoints serve content only from publicly readable buckets, which conflicts with Block Public Access, and a Referer header is easy to spoof.",
    "The legacy OAI does not support objects encrypted with SSE-KMS, so CloudFront could not read the files; OAC is the recommended replacement.",
    "OAC signs origin requests with SigV4 and supports SSE-KMS; the bucket policy allows the CloudFront service principal with an <code>AWS:SourceArn</code> condition for the distribution, and the key policy allows it to use the key.",
    "Signed URLs control which viewers can use the distribution; key groups are not IAM principals and cannot be granted bucket access, so the origin would still be unprotected."],
 ref:["d8e3","d8c3"], doc:"cloudfront"},

{id:"q08-032", day:8, dom:1, tags:["S3 security"],
 q:"A SaaS accounting application stores customer invoices as private PDF objects in an S3 bucket. Signed-in users must be able to download only their own invoices directly from S3, each download link must stop working after 15 minutes, and users must never receive AWS credentials. The application does not use CloudFront, and the team wants to add as little new infrastructure as possible. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Have the application back end check ownership and then generate an S3 presigned GET URL for the object that expires in 15 minutes.",
    "Add a bucket policy that allows <code>s3:GetObject</code> only when the <code>aws:Referer</code> header matches the application's domain.",
    "Put a CloudFront distribution in front of the bucket and have the application issue CloudFront signed URLs that expire in 15 minutes.",
    "Create an S3 access point for each customer and give each customer the access point alias so they can download their invoices."],
 a:[0],
 x:["A presigned URL carries the back end's permission and an expiry time, so the user downloads one object directly from S3 without credentials and the link stops working after 15 minutes.",
    "The Referer header is set by the client and easy to forge, and it can neither limit users to their own invoices nor expire links.",
    "Signed URLs would work, but a new CloudFront distribution is the extra infrastructure the team wants to avoid when S3 presigned URLs meet the need directly.",
    "Access points still require requests signed with AWS credentials, and one per customer adds infrastructure without providing expiring links."],
 ref:["d8e3","d4b1"], doc:"s3"},

{id:"q08-033", day:8, dom:1, tags:["Instance access","VPC endpoints & PrivateLink"],
 q:"Administrators connect to Linux EC2 instances in private subnets through a bastion host in a public subnet that allows SSH from the corporate IP range. A new security standard requires that no instance accept inbound connections on any port for administration, that SSH keys be retired, and that every session be logged to Amazon S3. The VPC has no NAT gateway but already has an S3 gateway endpoint. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Replace the bastion with an EC2 Instance Connect Endpoint, allow inbound SSH from the endpoint's security group, and push one-time keys.",
    "Keep the bastion host, require MFA on the corporate VPN, rotate SSH keys monthly, and send the bastion's shell history to CloudWatch Logs.",
    "Use Systems Manager Session Manager with an instance profile, add the Systems Manager interface endpoints, and enable S3 session logging.",
    "Deploy AWS Client VPN into the VPC so administrators can SSH directly to the private IP addresses, and log VPN connections to CloudWatch."],
 a:[2],
 x:["EC2 Instance Connect Endpoint removes the bastion, but the instances must still accept inbound SSH on port 22 from the endpoint, which the standard forbids.",
    "The bastion still requires inbound SSH and SSH keys, so it fails the new standard.",
    "The SSM Agent makes outbound connections to Systems Manager through the interface endpoints, so no inbound ports or SSH keys are needed, and session logs reach S3 through the gateway endpoint.",
    "Client VPN still relies on SSH keys and inbound port 22 on the instances, and connection logs do not record what happens inside each session."],
 ref:["d8e3","d2b3","d8b1"]},

{id:"q08-034", day:8, dom:1, tags:["WAF, Shield & Firewall Manager","Governance"],
 q:"A company has more than 100 AWS accounts in AWS Organizations, and application teams create new Application Load Balancers every week. The security team must ensure that every internet-facing ALB in every account, including accounts created in the future, is protected by a baseline set of AWS WAF rules that teams cannot remove. Which solution meets these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
 o:["Use AWS Firewall Manager with a WAF security policy scoped to the organization that applies the baseline rule groups to all ALBs automatically.",
    "Write a CloudFormation StackSet that creates a web ACL in each account, and ask teams to associate it with every new ALB they deploy.",
    "Attach an SCP that denies creating a load balancer unless the request also associates a web ACL with the load balancer in the same call.",
    "Enable AWS Config in every account with a rule that flags ALBs without a web ACL, and email the owning team to fix each finding."],
 a:[0],
 x:["Firewall Manager centrally deploys and enforces WAF rules across the organization, automatically covering new accounts and new in-scope resources and remediating noncompliant ones.",
    "StackSets can create the web ACLs, but association depends on each team remembering, and nothing stops a team from removing it later.",
    "Web ACL association is a separate API action from creating the load balancer, so an SCP cannot require it at creation time; this would simply block teams from creating ALBs.",
    "Config only detects missing web ACLs and relies on manual follow-up, which is slow and does not prevent removal."],
 ref:["d8e3","d4c3","d4c2"], doc:"waf"},

// ---------- Task 1.3: Determine appropriate data security controls ----------

{id:"q08-035", day:8, dom:1, tags:["KMS & encryption","S3 security"],
 q:"A legal firm stores case files in Amazon S3. Its compliance policy requires that the firm control who can use the encryption key separately from who can access the bucket, that every use of the key be logged with the identity of the caller, and that the key material rotate automatically each year. The firm wants AWS to perform the encryption. Which encryption option meets these requirements?",
 o:["SSE-S3 with Amazon S3 managed keys, with S3 server access logging enabled on the bucket.",
    "SSE-KMS with the AWS managed key <code>aws/s3</code>, with CloudTrail logging enabled for the account.",
    "SSE-C with keys that the firm generates, rotates every year and supplies with each request.",
    "SSE-KMS with a customer managed key that has a restrictive key policy and automatic rotation."],
 a:[3],
 x:["With SSE-S3 the firm cannot control key permissions separately from bucket access, and key usage is not logged per caller in CloudTrail.",
    "Use of the AWS managed key is logged, but its key policy cannot be edited, so the firm cannot control who uses the key separately from S3 permissions.",
    "SSE-C makes the firm generate, store, rotate and send keys with every request, and AWS keeps no key usage log for these customer-provided keys.",
    "A customer managed key lets the firm write the key policy, logs every Encrypt, Decrypt and GenerateDataKey call in CloudTrail, and supports automatic yearly rotation."],
 ref:["d8e4","d4b4","d8c3"], doc:"kms"},

{id:"q08-036", day:8, dom:1, tags:["KMS & encryption","IAM roles & cross-account"],
 q:"A data platform in Account A stores objects in an S3 bucket encrypted with SSE-KMS using a customer managed key in Account A. A reporting role in Account B must read the objects. The bucket policy already grants the Account B role <code>s3:GetObject</code>, and the role's identity policy allows <code>s3:GetObject</code> on the bucket, but downloads still fail with an access denied error from AWS KMS. Which combination of steps will resolve the issue? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Update the key policy in Account A to allow the Account B role, or Account B, to use <code>kms:Decrypt</code> on the key.",
    "Re-encrypt the objects with the AWS managed key <code>aws/s3</code> so that Account B can decrypt them without key policy changes.",
    "Enable S3 Bucket Keys on the bucket so that S3 decrypts objects for cross-account principals automatically.",
    "Add <code>kms:Decrypt</code> on the Account A key's ARN to the identity-based policy of the reporting role in Account B.",
    "Export the key material from Account A and import it into a new customer managed key in Account B."],
 a:[0,3],
 x:["Cross-account use of a KMS key requires the key policy in the owning account to allow the external principal; IAM policies in Account B alone are not enough.",
    "The key policy of an AWS managed key cannot be changed, so it cannot be shared with another account; objects encrypted with it cannot be decrypted cross-account.",
    "Bucket Keys reduce KMS request volume and cost, but they do not change who is authorized to use the key.",
    "Account B must also delegate permission to its own principal, so the role's identity policy needs <code>kms:Decrypt</code> on the specific key ARN.",
    "KMS does not allow key material it generated to be exported, and a different KMS key cannot decrypt ciphertext produced under the original key."],
 ref:["d8e4","d8c2","d4b4"], doc:"kms"},

{id:"q08-037", day:8, dom:1, tags:["S3 features","KMS & encryption"],
 q:"A company replicates an S3 bucket in eu-west-1 to a bucket in eu-central-1 with S3 Cross-Region Replication. Objects encrypted with SSE-S3 replicate, but objects encrypted with SSE-KMS using a customer managed key are never replicated. Compliance requires that the replicas be encrypted with a customer managed key in eu-central-1. What should a solutions architect do?",
 o:["Enable S3 Replication Time Control on the rule so that the KMS-encrypted objects replicate within 15 minutes.",
    "Opt the rule in to replicating SSE-KMS objects, set a destination key in eu-central-1, and let the role use both keys.",
    "Change the source objects to SSE-S3 encryption so that they replicate, and rely on the destination's default encryption.",
    "Enable S3 Bucket Keys on the destination bucket so that S3 can decrypt the source objects during replication."],
 a:[1],
 x:["Replication Time Control sets a time target for objects that are already eligible for replication; it does not make SSE-KMS objects eligible.",
    "SSE-KMS objects replicate only when the rule explicitly includes them and names a destination KMS key, and the replication role needs <code>kms:Decrypt</code> on the source key and <code>kms:Encrypt</code> on the destination key.",
    "Switching the source to SSE-S3 removes the control that the customer managed key provides and means rewriting every object, which is not what compliance asks for.",
    "Bucket Keys reduce KMS request costs; they do not give the replication role any permission on the source key."],
 ref:["d8e4","d3w1"], doc:"s3"},

{id:"q08-038", day:8, dom:1, tags:["EBS & instance store","KMS & encryption"],
 q:"A company's security policy requires that every new EBS volume and snapshot in its production account be encrypted with a specific customer managed KMS key. Engineers create volumes from the console, the CLI, launch templates and third-party tools, and the security team wants encryption to happen automatically without relying on each engineer. Which solution meets these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
 o:["Deploy the AWS Config encrypted-volumes managed rule and notify the owning engineer through Amazon SNS when a volume is unencrypted.",
    "Update every launch template to enable encryption on all block device mappings and to reference the customer managed key.",
    "Create an Amazon Data Lifecycle Manager policy that takes encrypted snapshots of all volumes every day using the customer managed key.",
    "Enable EBS encryption by default in each Region that the account uses, and set the customer managed key as the default key."],
 a:[3],
 x:["Config detects unencrypted volumes after they are created; it does not encrypt them, so the policy is violated until someone acts.",
    "Launch templates cover only instances launched from them; volumes created from the console, the CLI or other tools would still be unencrypted.",
    "Data Lifecycle Manager automates snapshot schedules; it does not encrypt the volumes themselves, so data at rest on the volumes stays unencrypted.",
    "Encryption by default is a per-Region account setting that encrypts every new volume, and the snapshots taken from it, with the chosen key, regardless of how the volume is created."],
 ref:["d8e4","d8c3","d4w1"], doc:"ebs"},

{id:"q08-039", day:8, dom:1, tags:["Encryption in transit","CloudFront"],
 q:"A company runs a web application behind an ALB in eu-west-1 with an ACM certificate for www.example.com. To improve global performance, a solutions architect is adding an Amazon CloudFront distribution with the alternate domain name www.example.com and HTTPS for viewers. The existing certificate does not appear in the list of certificates that can be selected for the distribution. What should the solutions architect do?",
 o:["Request a public ACM certificate for www.example.com in the us-east-1 Region and associate it with the distribution.",
    "Request the certificate again in eu-west-1 using email validation instead of DNS validation, and refresh the distribution.",
    "Keep the default *.cloudfront.net certificate and create a Route 53 alias record for www.example.com to the distribution.",
    "Set the origin protocol policy to HTTPS only so that CloudFront presents the ALB's existing certificate to the viewers."],
 a:[0],
 x:["CloudFront can use ACM certificates only from us-east-1 (N. Virginia), so a certificate for the custom domain must be requested or imported there.",
    "The validation method is not the problem; CloudFront cannot use ACM certificates from any Region other than us-east-1.",
    "The default certificate covers only *.cloudfront.net names, so browsers would show a name mismatch error for www.example.com.",
    "The origin protocol policy controls the CloudFront-to-origin connection; viewers still need a certificate on the distribution for the custom domain."],
 ref:["d8e4","d8c3"], doc:"acm"},

{id:"q08-040", day:8, dom:1, tags:["Encryption in transit","Load balancing"],
 q:"A healthcare company's patient portal runs on EC2 instances behind an Application Load Balancer that uses path-based routing and an AWS WAF web ACL. A new compliance rule requires that patient data be encrypted in transit on every network hop, including between the load balancer and the instances. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Use an HTTPS listener with an ACM certificate on the ALB, and keep the target group on HTTP because traffic inside a VPC is private.",
    "Replace the ALB with a Network Load Balancer that passes TCP 443 through to the instances so that TLS terminates on the instances.",
    "Use an HTTPS listener with an ACM certificate on the ALB, and use an HTTPS target group with certificates installed on the instances.",
    "Enable KMS encryption on the instances' EBS volumes, and use an HTTPS listener with an ACM certificate on the load balancer."],
 a:[2],
 x:["The hop from the ALB to the instances would be plaintext HTTP, which violates the requirement to encrypt every hop.",
    "TLS passthrough on an NLB would encrypt end to end, but an NLB cannot do path-based routing or use an AWS WAF web ACL, which the portal requires.",
    "The ALB terminates viewer TLS with the ACM certificate and opens a new TLS connection to the HTTPS targets, so both hops are encrypted while path routing and WAF keep working.",
    "EBS encryption protects data at rest, not in transit, so the ALB-to-instance hop would still be unencrypted."],
 ref:["d8e4","d8c3"], doc:"elb"},

{id:"q08-041", day:8, dom:1, tags:["S3 features","S3 security"],
 q:"A brokerage firm must store trade confirmation records in Amazon S3 in a WORM format for 7 years to satisfy financial regulations. During that period no user, including administrators and the root user, may delete or overwrite the records or shorten the retention period. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Enable S3 Object Lock on a versioned bucket and apply a default retention of 7 years in governance mode.",
    "Enable S3 Object Lock on a versioned bucket and apply a default retention of 7 years in compliance mode.",
    "Enable versioning with MFA delete on the bucket and add a bucket policy that denies <code>s3:DeleteObject</code> to all users.",
    "Add a lifecycle rule that moves records to S3 Glacier Deep Archive after 1 day and expires them after 7 years."],
 a:[1],
 x:["In governance mode, users with the <code>s3:BypassGovernanceRetention</code> permission can delete objects or shorten retention, so administrators could still remove records.",
    "In compliance mode, no user, including the root user, can delete a locked object version or shorten its retention until the retention period ends.",
    "An administrator can edit or remove the bucket policy, and the root user can still delete versions with MFA, so this is not WORM protection.",
    "Lifecycle rules manage storage class and expiration; they do not prevent anyone from deleting or overwriting objects before the 7 years are up."],
 ref:["d8e4","d3w1"], doc:"s3"},

{id:"q08-042", day:8, dom:1, tags:["S3 features","S3 security"],
 q:"A media company keeps raw footage in Amazon S3 and wants every new object protected from deletion and overwrite for 90 days after upload, with the protection ending automatically. In rare cases, such as a legal request to remove content, a small group of security administrators must be able to delete a protected object early. Everyone else must be blocked. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Enable S3 Object Lock in compliance mode with a 90-day default retention, and give the security administrators full S3 permissions.",
    "Place a legal hold on every new object, and allow only the security administrators to remove legal holds from objects when needed.",
    "Enable S3 Object Lock in governance mode with a 90-day default retention, and grant only the administrators the bypass permission.",
    "Enable versioning with MFA delete on the bucket, and give the root user's MFA device to the security administrators for these cases."],
 a:[2],
 x:["Compliance mode cannot be bypassed by anyone, even with full permissions, so the administrators could not delete content early.",
    "Legal holds have no expiry; each hold would have to be removed manually, so protection would not end automatically after 90 days.",
    "Governance mode applies the 90-day retention automatically and lets only principals with <code>s3:BypassGovernanceRetention</code>, sending the bypass header, delete or shorten retention early.",
    "MFA delete protects versions only from permanent deletion and depends on the root user; sharing root's MFA device breaks accountability and gives no automatic 90-day window."],
 ref:["d8e4","d3w1"], doc:"s3"},

{id:"q08-043", day:8, dom:1, tags:["S3 features"],
 q:"Engineers at a design agency have accidentally overwritten and deleted important files in an S3 bucket several times. The agency wants to be able to recover any previous version of an object, and it wants an extra authentication factor to be required before anyone can permanently delete an object version or suspend this protection. Which combination of actions meets these requirements? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Enable S3 Object Lock in compliance mode with a 30-day default retention on the bucket.",
    "Configure S3 Cross-Region Replication to a bucket in another Region owned by the same account.",
    "Enable S3 Versioning on the bucket so that overwrites and deletes keep the earlier object versions.",
    "Add a lifecycle rule that moves noncurrent object versions to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval.",
    "Enable MFA delete on the bucket by using the root user's credentials and its MFA device."],
 a:[2,4],
 x:["Compliance mode blocks all deletion during retention, including legitimate cleanup, and does not require an extra factor, so it does not match what is asked.",
    "Replication copies objects to another Region for resilience but does not require MFA for deletion, and overwrites are replicated as new versions anyway.",
    "With versioning, an overwrite creates a new version and a delete adds a delete marker, so earlier versions can always be restored.",
    "Moving noncurrent versions to Glacier lowers storage cost but adds no protection against permanent deletion.",
    "MFA delete requires an MFA code to permanently delete a version or change the bucket's versioning state, and only the root user can enable it."],
 ref:["d8e4","d3b1","d3w1"], doc:"s3"},

{id:"q08-044", day:8, dom:1, tags:["KMS & encryption"],
 q:"A payment processor is moving a card-authorization application to AWS. The application performs cryptographic signing through the industry-standard PKCS#11 interface. Regulators require that keys be generated and stored in dedicated, single-tenant hardware security modules under the company's exclusive control, with AWS having no access to the key material. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Deploy an AWS CloudHSM cluster with HSMs in two Availability Zones, and use the CloudHSM PKCS#11 library in the application.",
    "Create a KMS customer managed key with imported key material, and have the application call the KMS Sign API for signing.",
    "Create a KMS multi-Region key with automatic rotation, and give only the application's role access in the key policy.",
    "Store the private signing key in AWS Secrets Manager encrypted with a customer managed key, and sign in application code."],
 a:[0],
 x:["CloudHSM provides single-tenant HSMs in your VPC that only you control, with PKCS#11, JCE and OpenSSL support; HSMs in two AZs keep the cluster highly available.",
    "Imported key material still lives in the multi-tenant HSM fleet that KMS manages, and KMS exposes its own API rather than a PKCS#11 interface.",
    "Multi-Region keys replicate KMS keys across Regions, but they still use the shared KMS HSMs managed by AWS and do not offer PKCS#11.",
    "The private key would be loaded into application memory and used in software, not kept inside an HSM as the regulators require."],
 ref:["d8e4","d4w1"], doc:"kms"},

{id:"q08-045", day:8, dom:1, tags:["AWS Backup","Organizations & SCPs"],
 q:"A bank must retain backups of its Amazon RDS databases, EBS volumes and DynamoDB tables for 7 years across 40 AWS accounts in its organization. Backup schedules must be managed centrally, and regulators require that no one, including account administrators, can delete the backups or shorten their retention before it ends. Which combination of steps meets these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Set the automated backup retention period of every RDS DB instance to 7 years and enable deletion protection.",
    "Use AWS Backup backup policies in AWS Organizations to deploy backup plans with 7-year retention to all accounts.",
    "Create Amazon Data Lifecycle Manager policies in every account for EBS and use scripts for the other services.",
    "Export all snapshots to an S3 bucket with versioning enabled and a bucket policy that denies <code>s3:DeleteObject</code>.",
    "Apply AWS Backup Vault Lock in compliance mode to the backup vaults with a minimum retention of 7 years."],
 a:[1,4],
 x:["RDS automated backups can be retained for at most 35 days, and this does nothing for EBS or DynamoDB.",
    "Organizations backup policies push AWS Backup plans to every account from one place, covering RDS, EBS and DynamoDB in a single service.",
    "Per-account lifecycle policies and scripts are high-maintenance, are not centrally managed and offer no protection against deletion by administrators.",
    "Administrators can change the bucket policy, exporting every snapshot is extra work, and versioning alone does not make the backups immutable.",
    "Vault Lock in compliance mode makes the vault's retention rules immutable after the grace period; no user, including the root user, can delete recovery points early or shorten retention."],
 ref:["d8e4","d3p1"], doc:"backup"},

{id:"q08-046", day:8, dom:1, tags:["Encryption in transit","S3 security"],
 q:"An auditor found that some internal tools upload data to a company's S3 bucket over plain HTTP. The company's data protection standard requires that all access to the bucket be encrypted in transit, regardless of which application or principal makes the request. Which solution meets this requirement?",
 o:["Add a bucket policy statement that denies all S3 actions on the bucket when the <code>aws:SecureTransport</code> condition key is false.",
    "Enable default encryption on the bucket with SSE-KMS so that every object uploaded to the bucket is encrypted with a KMS key.",
    "Turn on S3 Block Public Access for the bucket so that only authenticated principals in the account can make requests to it.",
    "Put an Amazon CloudFront distribution in front of the bucket and set its viewer protocol policy to redirect HTTP to HTTPS."],
 a:[0],
 x:["<code>aws:SecureTransport</code> is false for requests made over HTTP, so an explicit deny on that condition rejects every unencrypted request to the bucket from any principal.",
    "Default encryption protects objects at rest after they arrive; it does not stop the data from traveling over unencrypted HTTP.",
    "Block Public Access prevents public grants, but authenticated principals could still send their requests over HTTP.",
    "Clients could still call the S3 endpoint directly over HTTP, bypassing CloudFront, so the bucket itself must enforce TLS."],
 ref:["d8e4","d8c3","d4c1"], doc:"s3"},

{id:"q08-047", day:8, dom:1, tags:["KMS & encryption"],
 q:"A genomics company is building an application that must encrypt files of 50 MB to 2 GB on the client side before uploading them to Amazon S3. The keys must be protected by AWS KMS, and the application must not send the large files to KMS. Which approach should the developers use?",
 o:["Call the KMS Encrypt API on each file in 4 KB chunks using the customer managed key, and upload the resulting encrypted chunks.",
    "Call the KMS GenerateDataKey API, encrypt each file locally with the plaintext data key, and store the encrypted data key with the file.",
    "Upload the plaintext files over HTTPS to an S3 bucket that has SSE-KMS default encryption with a customer managed key.",
    "Create a KMS key with imported key material, retrieve the key into the application, and use it to encrypt each file locally."],
 a:[1],
 x:["KMS Encrypt accepts at most 4 KB of data per call, and chunking large files through KMS would be slow, costly and throttled; KMS keys are meant to protect data keys, not bulk data.",
    "Envelope encryption uses a KMS-generated data key to encrypt data locally; only the small encrypted data key is stored with the file and sent to KMS for decryption later.",
    "SSE-KMS is server-side encryption that S3 performs after receiving the plaintext, so it does not meet the client-side encryption requirement.",
    "Key material never leaves KMS, whether it was generated by KMS or imported, so the application cannot retrieve the KMS key to use locally."],
 ref:["d8e4","d4w1","d4b4"], doc:"kms"},

{id:"q08-048", day:8, dom:1, tags:["KMS & encryption","DynamoDB"],
 q:"A global payments company encrypts sensitive fields in its application before writing them to a DynamoDB global table that replicates between eu-west-1 and us-east-1. The encryption currently uses a KMS key in eu-west-1. The company wants the application in us-east-1 to decrypt the fields by calling KMS only in us-east-1, so that a Regional outage in eu-west-1 does not stop decryption. What should a solutions architect do?",
 o:["Keep the single-Region key, and have the us-east-1 application call KMS in eu-west-1 over a cross-Region private connection to decrypt.",
    "Create a new KMS key in us-east-1 with the same alias as the eu-west-1 key so the application can refer to the key by alias in both Regions.",
    "Encrypt with a KMS multi-Region primary key in eu-west-1, create a replica key in us-east-1, and decrypt with the local key in each Region.",
    "Enable automatic key rotation on the eu-west-1 key so that KMS distributes the new versions of the key material to the other Regions."],
 a:[2],
 x:["This keeps a cross-Region dependency, so an outage in eu-west-1 would still stop decryption in us-east-1.",
    "An alias is only a friendly name; a new key has different key material and cannot decrypt ciphertext produced by the eu-west-1 key.",
    "Multi-Region keys share the same key ID and key material, so ciphertext created in one Region can be decrypted with the replica in the other Region without cross-Region calls.",
    "Rotation creates new key material for the same key in its own Region only; KMS keys are Regional, and rotation does not replicate them."],
 ref:["d8e4","d4b4"], doc:"kms"},

{id:"q08-049", day:8, dom:1, tags:["KMS & encryption","S3 security"],
 q:"An IoT analytics company writes millions of small objects per hour to an S3 bucket that uses SSE-KMS with a customer managed key. KMS request costs have grown sharply, and the application occasionally receives KMS throttling errors during peaks. The security team requires that the objects stay encrypted with the same customer managed key. Which solution meets these requirements with the LEAST effort?",
 o:["Switch the bucket's default encryption to SSE-S3, and keep using the customer managed key only for the objects that need it.",
    "Enable S3 Bucket Keys on the bucket so that S3 uses a bucket-level key from KMS and makes far fewer requests to KMS.",
    "Request a KMS request quota increase, keep the current configuration, and accept the KMS request costs as they grow.",
    "Encrypt objects on the client side with a single locally cached data key, and upload them with no server-side encryption."],
 a:[1],
 x:["SSE-S3 would remove the KMS costs but breaks the requirement to keep encrypting with the customer managed key.",
    "S3 Bucket Keys obtain a short-lived bucket-level key from KMS and use it to create object data keys, cutting KMS requests and cost substantially while still using the customer managed key.",
    "A quota increase may reduce throttling, but costs keep rising with request volume, and it does not address the root cause.",
    "This requires rewriting the application and managing client-side encryption, and reusing one data key indefinitely weakens security; it is far more effort."],
 ref:["d8e4","d4b4"], doc:"s3"},

{id:"q08-050", day:8, dom:1, tags:["Encryption in transit","RDS"],
 q:"A company's application connects to an Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL DB instance that is already encrypted at rest with a KMS key. An audit found that some clients connect without TLS. The company must ensure that the database rejects any connection that is not encrypted in transit, regardless of how each client is configured. What should a solutions architect do?",
 o:["Restrict the database security group to accept connections only on port 443 from the application's security group.",
    "Move the DB instance into private subnets with no route to the internet gateway so that traffic stays inside the VPC.",
    "Rotate the KMS key that encrypts the DB instance, and enable Performance Insights to monitor client connections.",
    "Set the <code>rds.force_ssl</code> parameter to 1 in a custom DB parameter group that is associated with the DB instance."],
 a:[3],
 x:["A security group filters by port and source; changing the port does not make PostgreSQL require TLS, and clients could still connect unencrypted.",
    "Private subnets reduce exposure but do not encrypt connections, so unencrypted clients inside the VPC could still connect.",
    "Key rotation concerns encryption at rest, and Performance Insights monitors database load; neither enforces TLS on connections.",
    "With <code>rds.force_ssl</code> set to 1, RDS for PostgreSQL rejects connections that do not use SSL/TLS; a custom group is needed because default groups cannot be edited (the default is 1 only on PostgreSQL 15 and later)."],
 ref:["d8e4","d8c3"], doc:"rds"}

);
